+# Narrowing the Veterinarian Directory by an Uncached Repository Query
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-004` narrows both forms of the veterinarian directory to the veterinarians holding one named specialty, matching the whole name and ignoring letter case. The page stays paged, and its page links carry the specialty forward.
+
+Two facts about the existing code constrain how the narrowing is realized. The page's totals must count the narrowed set rather than the whole directory, so the narrowing has to happen before the page is cut. And both of `VetRepository`'s read methods are cached in the `vets` cache, which is created with no size limit and no eviction — [Open Questions from the Survey](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) records that as undecided. Every route is unauthenticated, so a specialty name reaching that cache's key space is a caller-controlled key on a cache that never gives memory back.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Match in memory over the cached unfiltered read, and cut the page by hand.** Rejected: it reuses the cache but moves the matching rule and the paging arithmetic into `VetController`, which the pattern catalog's *Web controller* row forbids for a new rule, and it discards the paging the repository already gives.
+2. **Add a specialty-matching query and cache it in `vets` alongside the unfiltered reads.** Rejected: the cache key then carries a caller-supplied string, and the cache has neither a size limit nor eviction. Repeated requests naming distinct specialties grow it without bound.
+3. **Add a specialty-matching query and leave it out of the cache** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+`VetRepository` gains specialty-matching read methods — one paged for the page, one unpaged for the machine-readable form. They match the whole specialty name and compare it without regard to letter case in the query itself, not by relying on a column collation. Neither is cached.
+
+The two unfiltered reads keep their cache annotations and their behavior unchanged, which is what `REQ-VET-004` requires of a request naming no specialty.
+
+Deciding whether a specialty is named at all is not part of the query. A blank name narrows nothing, and that rule is one immutable value type in the `vet` package, used by both routes, testable without the web layer.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A narrowed request always reaches the database. The directory is small and the query is indexed by the join tables, so the cost is a query per narrowed request rather than a cached read.
+- Case-insensitive matching holds identically on H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, because the query states it. This is the same trap that made PostgreSQL owner search case-sensitive — see [Known Defects](../system-design.md#known-defects) — and stating the comparison in the query is what keeps the new behavior off that list.
+- The `vets` cache keeps a key space fixed by the page number alone, so the open question about its eviction stays as narrow as it is today.
+- If the narrowed directory later needs caching, it needs a bounded cache first. That is a separate decision, and this one does not pre-approve it.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003, REQ-VET-004
+
+- [PRD `REQ-VET-004`](../prd.md#req-vet-004) — the narrowing this realizes.
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the `VetRepository` and `VetController` rows.
+- [ADR: Feature-Package Organization Without a Service Layer](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md) — why the controller reaches the repository directly.
+- [ADR: Veterinarian Free-Text Search Stays Out](2026-08-24-non-goal-veterinarian-free-text-search.md) — the product decision admitting the filter.
